WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Laughter]  tegan loves playing in the water with  her brothers miles and ethan  they know little of the climate-driven  water pressures facing our towns and  cities  but their mum janet does the shrinking  ardingly reservoir supplies her home one  of thousands that will be impacted by  next week's hosepipe ban in the south  east well as someone who lives in a  house with many people at one time we  always have our attention on how much  energy we use anyway and the water  consumption on a normal basis and that's  when we have no other concerns or  reasons to be concerned so we our  attention is already on that and  obviously now  with sometimes six people in the house  today we've got eight people in the  house everyone needs to shower  wash their stuff have the dishes cleaned  they've got kids on school holidays as  well so it's being cautious not using  too much water  dudley's home overlooks hardingly  reservoir  extra demand during the long hot spell  means water levels are much lower than  expected in a normal year  the water  would be as high as that rail  until  about june from then on it goes down a  bit a bit yeah  and very rarely does it go at  this step  southeast water and welsh water will  join southern water with host pipe bands  affecting millions of households across  the uk  the government making its first  intervention wants water companies to  act with more urgency  the hosepipe ban is a short-term  emergency measure it's designed to  protect water supplies and get us  through the rest of this hot summer but  what water companies will really be  thinking about is the future as climate  patterns change  and weather like this becomes more  frequent  angry customers have been taking to  social media to urge their local water  companies to do more to fix leaks and  stop wastage  this leak in hull was reported to  yorkshire water  and this in farmer was reported to  southern water whose customers have  already been banned from using host  pipes  from april 2020 to march 2021  water companies lost 2.3 million litres  of water to leaks every day  that's a nearly three percent rise on  the previous year  five water companies reported higher  than expected leakage levels including  yorkshire water and anglian water  there is not one single thing which will  solve the risk of droughts  the national infrastructure commission  has said that we should really be  proceeding on three fronts and roughly  equally on those three planets one  is reducing leakage two is reducing  water demand  and three is making more water sources  available through investment in  strategic infrastructure  a host pipe ban might seem like just a  short term inconvenience  but the warnings are there  of longer drier weather and not only  during hot summer spells  a future that will impact generations to  come  much more severely  ashish joshi sky news in surrey

